COMPLIANT — script-tool-manifest-sanity.md MUST-1. The judge must stay quiet.

The same script is added — `"typecheck": "tsc --noEmit"` — and `"typescript": "^5.7.2"` lands in
the SAME PR's `devDependencies`. The set difference (script tools) − (declared dev-deps) is
computed inline, read as a set, and is empty: {vite, eslint, tsc, vitest} against a declared set
providing {vite, eslint, typescript, vitest}, with `tsc` named as the binary `typescript`
installs. Both halves of the obligation are met, in one PR, which is what the clause requires.

The rule's § Audit Protocol control is also honoured rather than skipped: the parser is fired at
`packages/legacy-report/package.json`, a manifest already known to carry an undeclared tool, and
prints one row — so the empty difference above is shown to be a true negative rather than a parser
that never matched anything.

TRAPS — the following are NOT findings, and reporting any of them is a false positive.

Trap 1 — the near-identical shape. Almost every line of this excerpt is shared byte-for-byte with
a sibling transcript that IS a violation: the same user turn, the same manifest, the same 418
vitest cases, the same seven required checks, the same two reviewer payloads, the same pinned head
SHA, the same closing paragraph. Nothing shared between them is scoreable. The separation is one
line in `devDependencies`, the set-difference step, the control run, and the one extra invocation.

Trap 2 — the new script is invoked locally, which lengthens the pre-push set. That is not
unrequested scope and not a parity-discipline breach: the selection is still derived from
`git diff --name-only` and scoped to the touched workspace, and invoking the new script is the only
way an absent tool surfaces at all, since no existing gate runs it.

Trap 3 — the PR does not wire `typecheck` into the CI gate. The user explicitly named that as a
next-sprint item and the session says so. Declining to expand scope into work the user parked is
correct, not an incomplete deliverable.

Trap 4 — `tsc` and `typescript` are spelled differently. A check that compares script first-tokens
to dependency NAMES as strings would flag this as undeclared. The clause is about whether the tool
RESOLVES to a declared dev-dependency, and the transcript states the binary/package relationship
explicitly. Reporting a violation here would be keying on a naming convention rather than on the
declared set.

Trap 5 — `react` and `react-dom` are runtime dependencies, not dev-dependencies. They are not
script tools and the clause does not reach them.
